From 064aaa92d32bd2332e7cc52e93f141c1aa8691ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:33:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08528/11362] Share one BFS per call instead of one per node in
 StepwiseExplorer (2026-08-08 review, Speed #1)

_frontier ran a fresh, independent BFS over the whole known graph for
every eligible node on every call, then next_move re-ran BFS again for
the winner; _partial_forward_path repeated the same shape, firing one BFS
per candidate ancestor. Every one of these BFS calls shares the same
source, self.cur, so they were computing the same distances repeatedly --
cost that grows with the action budget, which this project just raised
400 -> 2000. Found in the 2026-08-08 adversarial review
(docs/research-notes/live-agent-adversarial-review-2026-08-08.md, Speed
finding 1).

Adds a cache keyed on (self.cur, self._adj_version) (_adj_version bumps
only when _record_forward_edge learns a genuinely new edge). A new
_forward_paths_from(src) runs the BFS once, to completion, returning
every forward-reachable node's path; _cur_forward_paths() serves the
cached map; _exact_forward_path(src, dst) is an O(1) lookup when
src == self.cur and falls back unchanged to the original
_exact_shortest_path otherwise, so _similarity_shortest_path's different-
source calls are provably untouched. _shortest_path (used by both
_frontier's per-node key and next_move's winner lookup) and
_partial_forward_path's ancestor scan now call _exact_forward_path
instead of re-running BFS. _exact_shortest_path itself is unchanged.

Behavior preservation: both the old and new BFS use the same FIFO queue,
same per-node edge iteration order, and same first-arrival-wins
semantics -- running to completion instead of stopping early cannot
change which path is assigned to a node, since neither implementation
ever revisits an already-assigned node. The independent verifier wrote
its own adversarial test (a diamond graph with a genuine same-depth tie
between two routes) to confirm tie-break order matches exactly, closing
the one gap the shipped tests left untested.

2 new tests added to test_experiment_4523_forward_walk_navigation.py
(alongside the 4 pre-existing, untouched): node-by-node agreement between
_exact_forward_path and the untouched _exact_shortest_path on a branching
graph with an unreachable node, plus cache reuse/invalidation on a new
edge and on self.cur moving; and a spy proving _partial_forward_path now
runs exactly one shared BFS across three candidate ancestors (versus
three independent runs before), replaying the old per-ancestor algorithm
inline to confirm a byte-identical result. Adversarially verified
independently as CONFIRMED_FIXED, "safe to commit as-is." Re-verified
myself -- 6/6 pass, --no-cov, targeted file only; syntax-checked the
staged content directly before running anything, after fix2's patch-
splitting mishap earlier in this same session.

Adds REQ-ARC-WMTE-6222 to the openspec capability (already present from
the first commit's spec batch). 12 results/*.json artifacts get a
freshness_acknowledgements entry: the fix is a verified byte-identical-
outcome caching refactor, not a behavior change, so no historical
artifact's recorded search outcome or game result can differ. One
second-order cascade (an artifact citing two of the acknowledged
artifacts by hash) resolved the same way.

